  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA mid/upper-level high remains anchored over the Tennessee \nValley, southern Appalachians and Carolinas through Thursday, \nmaintaining the heat, but leaving some room on its northeast \nperiphery for short wave troughs/convective complexes to \npossibly graze northern / northeastern portions of the forecast \narea. There, modest but adequate deep layer bulk shear, 30 to 35\nkts at times associated with the various short waves, plentiful\nmoisture with PW values of 1.5 to 2 inches, and afternoon CAPE \nof 2 to 3 KJ/kg, could lead to storms becoming strong to even \nmarginally severe.\n\nIn the near term, the NAMNest shows complexes grazing northern \n/ northeastern portions of the forecast area this afternoon, \nwith additional scattered development farther south in the \nmiddle Ohio Valley, and again tonight, with deeper penetration \nsouthward into the forecast area before dissipating late \ntonight.\n\nThe HIRESWarw shows a convective complex moving southward into \nthe forecast area overnight tonight. The CAMs otherwise show \nconvective complexes remaining north of the area through \ntonight. For Thursday, most models suggest most of the area will\nbe dry most of the day, with isolated afternoon thunderstorms \nnorth.\n\nAll of this leaves the area mostly in the heat the next two\ndays, and the Heat Advisories have been expanded to now include\nalmost all of the lowlands, and has been extended in time to \ninclude Thursday.\n\nH85 temperatures peak around 20-21C the next two afternoons, \nwhich supports lowland high temperatures in the mid to upper 90s\nF. With dew points in the low 70s F, heat indices are likely to\nreach the low to mid 100s this and Thursday afternoon, the \nhighest values centered across the Huntington Tri-state and \nCharleston Metro areas. The Heat Advisories may need upgraded \nif confidence increases in Heat Index values reaching and \nexceeding 105 F in more than brief, isolated instances.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe ridge amplifies but retrogrades westward Friday, allowing s\nshort wave trough to drive a surface cold front toward the area\nfrom the northwest Thursday night into Friday, and then through\nthe area Friday afternoon and night, and possibly even beyond. \nThis increases the chance for / coverage of showers and storms \nand, eventually, a break in the oppressive heat. There is the \nthreat for strong, heavy thunderstorms as pooling of moisture\nnear the front increase CAPE over 3 KJ/kg and PW values over 2 \ninches, and bulk shear through 6 to 8 km increases to 40 to 50 \nkts associated with individual mid-level ripples riding along \nthe front.\n\nCurrent timing suggests the greatest threat is still northern /\nnortheastern portions of the area late Thursday afternoon and \nevening working southward before diminishing late Thursday night,\nand then the bulk of the forecast area being impacted Friday \nafternoon and evening.\n\nThe Storm Prediction Center maintains a Slight Risk for Severe \nThunderstorms for northern into central portions of the forecast\narea for Thursday afternoon and evening, with a Marginal Risk \nfor central into southern portions of the area for Thursday \nafternoon and night. The Storm Prediction Center shifts the \nSlight Risk for Severe Thunderstorms to southeastern portions of\nthe area for Friday afternoon and evening, with a Marginal Risk\nfor a less focused threat elsewhere.\n\nWhile Heavy Rainfall can become excessive, it appears any Flash\nFlood threat will be localized/isolated, and transient.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nModel solutions have come in closer agreement on at least the \nsurface cold front exiting the area to the south and west \nSaturday, as well as a subtle mid-level short wave moving \nsoutheast in the northwest flow on the east side of the then \namplified ridge. That short wave will either cross the area or \nslide to the southwest of the area Saturday. Even if it crosses \nthe area, the cooler air behind the front should keep the Severe\nWeather and even Excessive Rainfall threat little to none amid\nits associated showers and possible afternoon thunderstorms.\n\nSurface high pressure eventually builds south from eastern \nCanada behind the cold front, with more seasonable temperatures \nbringing a break in the oppressive heat Saturday and drier \nweather arriving by Sunday.\n\nOnce in control, surface high pressure maintains control through\nthe balance of the Labor Day Holiday weekend including Labor \nDay itself, and into the upcoming short work week. Meanwhile, \nmid/upper-level heights start to rebuild as the ridge slides \neast while deamplifying somewhat. This keeps mainly dry weather \ngoing, with a renewed warming trend possibly commencing by \nmidweek.",
